{
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "tracking_frame",
      "description": "The failures occurred while pressing both buttons and tracking the containers hiding the requested colors. In several clips, the first newly shown frame preserved the preceding anchor's supported, localized target geometry, and later frames showed an exchange with unresolved endpoint localization. The policy selected the appropriate button retraction or closing waypoint but retained the old anchor in addition to the entire new clip; the annotation instead discarded that old anchor in favor of the first unchanged new frame. A related failure occurred after closing around the first requested container: the policy correctly selected a lift but retained the approach-to-grasp trajectory, whereas the annotation compressed to the current grasp frame. These are PREDICTABILITY failures in anchor selection, not missing action history or waypoint rules.\n\nClarify the existing ordered trajectory-keyframe rule as a frame-by-frame operation. After incorporating successful pickups, evaluate each newly shown frame chronologically for all still-needed targets. At each frame, use only evidence available through that frame. If positively unchanged visible supported geometry and correspondence carry reliable localization from the preceding evidence, that frame qualifies even without a new metric measurement or a change to target_locations. Save the latest qualifying frame and all subsequent shown frames. Later movement or unresolved endpoint localization does not retroactively disqualify an earlier qualifying frame: it prevents compression to the later unresolved endpoint, not advancement to an earlier unchanged frame in the same clip. For example, if the incoming anchor is E54, E55 positively preserves its localized geometry, and E71 and E87 show unresolved motion, write [\"E55\", \"E71\", \"E87\"], not [\"E54\", \"E55\", \"E71\", \"E87\"].\n\nAt grasp closure, explicitly apply the existing partial-occlusion test rather than treating finger occlusion as automatic anchor failure. Exposed opposing outer edges or reconstructible outer corners in the current wrist image, combined with front-view continuity establishing identity and unchanged table support, can establish the grasped target's reliable localization. If every other still-needed target also remains visibly localized with unambiguous correspondence, replace the trajectory with the current label alone, for example [\"E289\"], even when every target_locations entry remains unchanged. If that geometry or support continuity is genuinely unavailable, retain the last qualifying anchor and subsequent trajectory. Do not relax the existing prohibitions on inheriting localization through an exchange, unresolved elevation change, or target invisibility."
    }
  ]
}

